Create Group 04 — Navigation Controls for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives.

Existing groups:

Group 01 — Interaction Controls
Group 02 — Form Controls
Group 03 — Selection Controls

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine any global design token.

Inherit all:
- color tokens
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- sizing
- control heights
- borders
- radius
- elevation
- shadows
- iconography
- motion
- breakpoints
- responsive behavior
- accessibility
- focus behavior
- touch-target rules
- density
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast
- RTL
- localization
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency

Create exactly these 10 components:

031 Tabs
032 Tab Bar
033 Breadcrumbs
034 Pagination
035 Stepper
036 Navigation Menu
037 Navigation Item
038 Bottom Navigation
039 Anchor Navigation
040 Previous / Next Navigation

These must be production-grade reusable navigation primitives.

Do not create:
- complete application sidebars
- dashboards
- website headers
- application shells
- documentation layouts
- complete navigation systems
- page templates
- marketing navigation sections

Those are higher-level compositions.

Every navigation component must communicate:

1. Where the user currently is.
2. What destinations are available.
3. What is selectable versus navigational.
4. Which item is active.
5. What happens when an item is activated.

Do not use color alone to communicate the active state.

Use appropriate:
- active indicators
- typography
- position
- icons
- underlines
- surface changes
- semantic attributes

depending on the component.

All components must support:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop
Ultra Wide

All components must support:

Light
Dark
High Contrast

All components must support:

Mouse
Keyboard
Touch
Stylus

where relevant.

All components must support:

LTR
RTL
Localization
Dynamic text scaling
Reduced motion
Reduced transparency

All interactive navigation targets must provide a minimum effective touch target of 44×44px.

Navigation controls must not create accidental page overflow.

Long labels must be handled intelligently.

Do not blindly truncate navigation labels.

Where space becomes constrained, use appropriate responsive transformation such as:
- wrapping
- scrolling
- collapsing
- overflow menu
- alternate navigation presentation

depending on the component.

Do not simply scale text smaller to solve navigation overflow.

Preserve readable typography.

Navigation hierarchy must be visually obvious.

Primary navigation must receive stronger hierarchy than secondary navigation.

Active navigation must be unmistakable.

Disabled navigation items must remain understandable without becoming unreadably faint.

Keyboard focus must always remain visible.

Navigation must support browser/platform conventions.

Do not interfere with standard:
- back
- forward
- open in new tab/window
- copy link
- keyboard navigation

behavior unnecessarily.

Links should remain actual links where navigation semantics require links.

Buttons should be used for actions.

Do not use button semantics for normal page navigation merely because styling is convenient.

For composite navigation controls:
provide appropriate semantic roles and relationships.

Do not create nested interactive controls.

Navigation items containing icons must have meaningful icons.

Do not use decorative icons simply to make the navigation appear richer.

Icons must not replace text when the meaning would become ambiguous.

Do not use excessive animation.

Navigation transitions should feel responsive and subtle.

The active indicator should transition quickly and naturally.

Avoid:
- excessive gradients
- heavy shadows
- oversized borders
- unnecessary glass effects
- decorative backgrounds
- excessive rounded containers

Navigation should feel:
calm,
precise,
hierarchical,
premium,
fast,
predictable,
and effortless.

Responsive behavior must be intentional.

Do not simply shrink desktop navigation into a tiny mobile version.

Mobile navigation should be designed around:
thumb reach
limited width
touch targets
scrolling
safe areas
gesture conflicts

Desktop navigation should prioritize:
information density
keyboard access
pointer interaction
hover feedback
efficient switching

All components must be composable with existing DevKit primitives.

Test:
short labels
long labels
many destinations
few destinations
localized labels
RTL
large text
high zoom
keyboard-only operation
screen readers
touch
narrow mobile widths
wide desktop widths
disabled destinations
active destinations
deep navigation structures

No component may cause unexpected layout shifts.
